WEBVTT

NOTE automatic transcription, uncorrected

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
What is the most important question that one should ask about Cedric?

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.556
Well, I can tell you the... I don't know whether it's the most important, but it's

00:00:08.556 --> 00:00:10.000
the one that gets asked...

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:17.000
I get asked most frequently, which is whether Cedric wanted to build or not.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:23.529
And the sort of urban myth is that no, he wasn't really interested in building,

00:00:23.529 --> 00:00:27.000
because so much of the work is unrealised.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.840
And I would completely dispute that.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.060
I think he absolutely wanted to build.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.440
But if you read through the stories of every single project

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.880
and what goes on around every project,

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:47.540
in every case, for very many different reasons in each instance,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.580
Cedric has a very good reason not to complete the project

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.720
or the project is abandoned for one reason or another.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:01.258
So I think those two things get conflated and it's presumed that he never wanted to

00:01:01.258 --> 00:01:03.700
build and I think that's wrong.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:09.450
So I'm always glad that question is asked and I think it's an important one to get

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:09.860
right.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:15.281
He opened my eyes and this refers back to the experience of understanding what I was

00:01:15.281 --> 00:01:18.780
doing could be described as architecture, what I was thinking.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:27.540
and so he was the opportunity for me to see the architecture was more than a

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:34.780
formal and exercises in formal rearrangement which is what I had

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:39.060
learnt in school and through studying history of art you know history of art

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:44.060
was studied through kind of sequence of styles and as far as I could see it

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:51.871
rearrangement of the same formal components. And learning about Cedric's work I

00:01:51.871 --> 00:01:52.860
understood

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:59.518
that it was conceivable, it could be an entirely different construct, which for him

00:01:59.518 --> 00:02:00.160
involved

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:06.677
time and speed and fundamentally human, but it was human-centric as opposed to

00:02:06.677 --> 00:02:07.680
form-centric

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.000
and that was really interesting to me.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:16.800
Always incredibly enthusiastic,

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.340
generous, funny,

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.000
really hard work,

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:25.680
incredibly...

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:27.840
Obtuse is the wrong word.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.560
Just fantastically good at taking conversations at a diversion,

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:36.180
which I'm sure lots of people will remark on.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.640
But his personality, certainly from my experience, was incredibly...

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:47.520
..listened intently to what you had to say and always had some response,

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.440
although the response was never quite...

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.160
It certainly was never what you expected

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.080
and never quite registered what he was saying at the time,

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.520
but then quite often, years later, you think,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.040
''Oh, that's what that was about.''

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.840
I was working in the office,

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.320
using the office as a place to work,

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:11.032
In 2000, year 2000, I'd visited it before when as a student, again Cedric was always

00:03:11.032 --> 00:03:17.743
very generous about saying, you know, if you want to come and discuss your work, the

00:03:17.743 --> 00:03:20.220
office closed at three o'clock.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:26.020
And after three o'clock you could make an appointment and come up to the office.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:32.379
So I visited once as a student and after three o'clock all meetings took place in

00:03:32.379 --> 00:03:34.420
the white room at the top.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.620
And that meant there was no telephone or no interruption.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:45.964
And during that meeting, again, I don't remember the specifics of the conversation,

00:03:45.964 --> 00:03:48.020
but it was just such a...

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:52.640
As a student, I suppose it was an enormous indulgence in one way.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.400
and he liked to indulge it and he wanted to have the conversation about the work.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:05.420
So it was, and it was a really good, in a way it was a test as a student.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:06.980
You know, you had to go and present your work.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:11.000
It wasn't for a job, it wasn't in a school context.

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:12.680
It was just to talk about your work.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:15.600
So that was a really unique thing.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:23.890
Then afterwards, from 2000, when I proposed to make a book of his work, following on

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:24.680
from the

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:30.003
square book that the AA had produced in 1984, which documented the first 20 years of

00:04:30.003 --> 00:04:30.700
the office,

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:36.937
that seemed to go out of print and I approached Cedric and said, we need to do, well

00:04:36.937 --> 00:04:37.680
one, could

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.588
we look at reprinting that and two, could we look at documenting the rest of the

00:04:43.588 --> 00:04:44.400
work and he

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.700
immediately said no he wasn't interested books take too long they're like

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:51.420
buildings but it's out of date before you've even finished it and it's a

00:04:51.420 --> 00:05:00.001
pointless activity so I went away and thought that's a bit mean so and then I went

00:05:00.001 --> 00:05:00.420
back

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:04.800
about a month later I said that's really mean and he said well give me a good

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.040
reason why you should do it and I said well because the drawers are full of

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.180
drawings and projects which should be seen and the only point of doing all of

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:17.332
this stuff is so other people can look at it, particularly students. I reckon

00:05:17.332 --> 00:05:17.800
they've

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:24.987
got a lot to learn from what you've been up to. So he relented and immediately

00:05:24.987 --> 00:05:25.540
opened

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:31.478
the drawer of his desk and he had a full layout of chapter headings and so he's

00:05:31.478 --> 00:05:32.380
clearly been

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:40.495
thinking about it actually. And that was Cedric actually in so many ways. Cedric's

00:05:40.495 --> 00:05:41.980
often described

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:49.800
as just a series or a crescendo of paradoxes in every way.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:56.020
Again, you know, from his appearance to his, also the way he operated,

00:05:56.980 --> 00:06:00.880
his appearance being extremely, on the one hand, very formal

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:06.260
and almost sort of Edwardian gent, but at the same time quite moddish.

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:09.640
And he was written about in the US newspapers in the 60s

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.360
as being this kind of mod Englishman.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:15.200
So he always was...

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.020
He was playing with that, absolutely,

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:23.060
and absolutely in control of what was going on there.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.180
And also in terms of the way he operated.

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:27.480
He kept every...

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.120
Again, from where I...

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.740
My understanding, and also having looked now at his diaries,

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.760
at the office diaries, and the way that they were laid out,

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.680
he kept every part of his life discreet from the other part.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:42.880
So his private life was very private.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:46.860
What happened between seven and nine in the morning

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:51.540
was a particular sequence of conversations with a particular set of people,

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:57.000
which then had no overlap with office hours of what occurred in the office.

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:02.660
Lunchtime then was also used for different kinds of meetings with clients,

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:05.600
or mostly clients actually.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:13.642
and then the afternoon then goes into another sort of area of research or other

00:07:13.642 --> 00:07:14.540
interests

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:20.124
or you know sort of peripheral activities which all culminated in projects one way

00:07:20.124 --> 00:07:20.260
or

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:26.059
another. But they're very very discrete and therefore kept people very separate. And

00:07:26.059 --> 00:07:26.680
similarly

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:33.640
with the situation of the contents coming out of the drawer, you know, he was always

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:37.436
working on one level or another. That's what Cedric did from, I guess, five in the

00:07:37.436 --> 00:07:37.760
morning

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:43.270
till two o'clock the following morning. I don't think he slept very much.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:44.100
Physically,

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:48.541
it was separated. You know, he had his office on the, his desk on the first floor

00:07:48.541 --> 00:07:48.980
and then

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:53.676
the white room was this completely other space, which the office members, I mean,

00:07:53.676 --> 00:07:54.140
when, if

00:07:54.140 --> 00:08:00.718
you speak to them, they'll, they were rarely invited except if it was a party or a

00:08:00.718 --> 00:08:01.520
particular

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:05.240
event but not in the day-to-day running that was absolutely where guests came

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:11.540
and on Saturday mornings he would have special sessions to discuss a project

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:18.040
and invite people up so everything was very very clearly organized in his mind

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:25.940
and in his diary and in that in that para and the paradox I guess lies in the

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:30.120
fact that the what he was constantly doing the work always but always gave

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:36.377
the appearance of being entirely spontaneous and never quite, and always questioning

00:08:36.377 --> 00:08:36.600
and

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:45.256
doubt being kind of fundamental to his philosophy. So that questioning I think often

00:08:45.256 --> 00:08:46.080
gave the

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:51.008
impression that he really wasn't particularly organised or knowing what was going on

00:08:51.008 --> 00:08:51.360
there,

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:54.600
but it was completely the opposite.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:09:00.751
Well I think delight again it comes back to this sort of human centric idea of what

00:09:00.751 --> 00:09:01.640
architecture

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:10.020
was useful for. It was potentially it was useful as a source of human delight or it

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:18.600
should be, that's what its role should be and why shouldn't it be. And I think that

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:23.238
specifically in architecture that's something he tried to build in to what he

00:09:23.238 --> 00:09:23.780
designed.

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:31.223
There was always a, really often in most projects, there was such comical aspects to

00:09:31.223 --> 00:09:32.020
the, one,

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:38.723
the fact that often they were either dynamic, literally dynamic buildings, or the

00:09:38.723 --> 00:09:39.220
way he

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:47.040
thought about details in terms of entry or exit, or there were always, he was always

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:52.675
making observations about how that could be uplifting as a moment in engaging with

00:09:52.675 --> 00:09:53.500
architecture

00:09:53.500 --> 00:10:00.156
opposed to something just practically functional or utilitarian. Although

00:10:00.156 --> 00:10:01.980
aesthetically that's

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:07.715
often how they come across so I think again sometimes it gets lost but certainly in

00:10:07.715 --> 00:10:08.060
terms

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:13.499
of the architecture that's there in all of the drawings and always the fact that he

00:10:13.499 --> 00:10:14.220
peoples his

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:21.664
drawings in the projects. People are always doing funny things looking or you know

00:10:21.664 --> 00:10:22.300
they're

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:26.680
They're incredibly tender in the way that they're drawn.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:29.640
So there's delight in that respect.

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:31.860
And then just delight in his general well-being.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:36.420
He was extremely ebullient.

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:39.540
So I think that's a word, a way of describing,

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:44.180
as a character, you know, an amazing laugh.

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:46.180
Well, I think the one that really,

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:52.080
Fun Palace, I think, has to be.

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:55.580
I mean, it was more or less the first.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:57.660
It wasn't chronologically the first,

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:00.200
but it was very early on in his career.

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:05.080
And it set the tone and set the agenda

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:08.500
for so many aspects of all the projects that came after.

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:15.960
And various parts of that project were then developed through generators,

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:20.000
absolutely a by-product of Fun Palace, the Oxford Corner Houses,

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:26.060
magnet right at the end is also in lots of ways.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:30.660
So I think all roads do lead to Fun Palace really

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:31.920
in terms of its significance

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:36.460
and just the sheer size of the project

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:40.680
in terms of numbers of people involved,

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:43.380
aspects that he tried to engage in

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:47.240
in terms of the hardware of the building and the software,

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:49.640
the whole establishing of the cybernetics committee

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:52.580
and in terms of thinking about programming.

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:57.500
Fun Palace officially started in 1960.

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:01.800
I think there were conversations occurring a little prior to 1960.

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:10.120
Certainly Cedric and Jane Littlewood were introduced in about 1958 by Tom Dryberg.

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:15.760
The three of them then became the sort of core body that ran the project.

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:24.824
And then it finished in 1966, sort of officially, when they retracted their planning

00:12:24.824 --> 00:12:28.600
application from the Millmead site,

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:31.740
which is the site just below what we now know as the Olympic Park.

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:33.300
It's very close to Stratford.

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:38.480
They were looking at a number of different sites over a number of years,

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:43.760
always trying to, really trying to find the place for the Fun Palace.

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:46.960
That was a huge part of the work.

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:55.265
And Millmeads was the closest because it was offered as an opportunity to start to

00:12:55.265 --> 00:12:57.640
promote this by the Civic Trust,

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:02.605
who owned that land, that tract of land in East London, which is part of the Lee

00:13:02.605 --> 00:13:03.020
Valley.

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:07.420
And so they were very supportive of it.

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:15.359
in the end politically there was a shift there was a change in uh county hall and a

00:13:15.359 --> 00:13:16.220
change in

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:22.117
the political makeup of of london and the support and one week it's there the next

00:13:22.117 --> 00:13:23.340
week it's not and

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:29.837
it was as simple it was really as simple as that and there was a a challenge to the

00:13:29.837 --> 00:13:30.620
to the fun

00:13:30.620 --> 00:13:35.751
palace um that the site that it was going to be built on was very close to

00:13:35.751 --> 00:13:36.860
stormwater tanks

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:41.960
which would deal with flooding and also sewage outflow.

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:45.960
So it lost out to the sewage outflow tanks.

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:50.900
And Cedric and Joan of Lourdes quite rightly decided

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:56.680
that sewage outflow was more important than Fun Palace

00:13:56.680 --> 00:14:04.120
for Londoners, actually, which I think is big of them.

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:06.300
The Maccabee Project.

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:15.340
That was commissioned by Sir Alistair McAlpine, who was a friend of Cedric's.

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:24.860
And about ten years Cedric's junior, someone described him as Cedric's...

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:29.740
That Cedric had created the perfect client.

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:36.931
I think McAlpine was a very, he was an interesting man because he left school very

00:14:36.931 --> 00:14:44.209
young, he was a total autodidact, went straight into the family business I think at

00:14:44.209 --> 00:14:51.575
the age of about 15 or 16, working on the construction site and worked his way up to

00:14:51.575 --> 00:14:52.540
the office.

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:58.902
He was very adamant to his, all his uncles and father were working in the offices at

00:14:58.902 --> 00:15:00.720
an administrative level.

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:05.480
And he insisted he wanted to know the business from the construction site.

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:10.940
So he was interesting in that respect, I think, to Cedric.

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:19.416
And also on the part that he was part of this family, very large family construction

00:15:19.416 --> 00:15:20.080
company.

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:30.184
When the project happened in the 70s, it was at the time where the labour force was

00:15:30.184 --> 00:15:34.080
still very much unreconstructed.

00:15:35.140 --> 00:15:39.160
It was completely unregulated.

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:48.720
So a lot of the labourers were Irish labourers coming over, being paid cash in hand.

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:54.874
conditions were terrible and deaths on sites were just astronomical I mean they were

00:15:54.874 --> 00:15:55.460
in there

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:59.600
hundreds of thousands people would die on construction sites all over the country so

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:03.435
there's this very in the project files etc there's this great statistic and I

00:16:03.435 --> 00:16:04.580
haven't got the numbers

00:16:04.580 --> 00:16:10.380
on the tip of my tongue but there was this statistic of how many deaths in the year

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:10.660
that

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:14.906
project started had occurred in construction so that was really the for him I think

00:16:14.906 --> 00:16:15.520
the catalyst

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:22.745
for the project and then the other catalyst being that there were a number of sites

00:16:22.745 --> 00:16:23.180
under

00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:30.375
construction that McAlpines were responsible for and they were looking to make their

00:16:30.375 --> 00:16:31.060
business

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:40.109
more efficient. How could they save money and make money and Cedric saw it very much

00:16:40.109 --> 00:16:40.540
that

00:16:40.540 --> 00:16:45.727
it was a logistical exercise in many ways how to organize the construction sites so

00:16:45.727 --> 00:16:46.540
one it became

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:52.782
a safer place to work but also how it could become a more efficient place it was a

00:16:52.782 --> 00:16:54.000
specific case of

00:16:54.000 --> 00:17:01.011
of how to have a crane on site and not have to remove it and bring it back but how

00:17:01.011 --> 00:17:01.780
you could

00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:06.596
keep the crane on the site conducting a number of different maneuvers and not have

00:17:06.596 --> 00:17:07.360
to go through

00:17:07.360 --> 00:17:15.000
logistics of so in lots of ways extremely simple in the one hand perhaps

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:22.720
totally unrelated to architecture or the final product of architecture but

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:29.300
entirely related to the process of making architecture and again from the

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:37.060
human perspective in terms of how you make that both healthier and happier

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:42.060
mentally, physically and economically for everybody. So that was the impetus for

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:47.120
the project. It's very hard because I have lots actually but I think one of

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:53.480
one of my favorite drawings is for a project called Tertlan which is in

00:17:53.480 --> 00:18:02.973
in Groszny in Croatia. And it was one workshop Cedric did in the early 90s for a

00:18:02.973 --> 00:18:04.160
small hill

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:12.360
town. And he, the premise for the project was to, he did it in a week, so it's a set

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:19.073
of three or four drawings. But it was to, how to bring this very ancient hill town

00:18:19.073 --> 00:18:19.400
into

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:29.537
the 21st century. This is, as I said, very early 90s, so pre-broadband and internet

00:18:29.537 --> 00:18:30.880
connection,

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:36.809
although of course the notions were already very present. So Cedric designs this

00:18:36.809 --> 00:18:37.180
halo,

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:47.142
a physical halo around the village which is held up by three lifting cranes. And

00:18:47.142 --> 00:18:47.640
then

00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:52.552
With the advent of broadband coming in, I'm imagining he thinks it could just be

00:18:52.552 --> 00:18:52.920
driven

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:59.725
away or if you no longer decide you want to be linked to the rest of the world, it

00:18:59.725 --> 00:19:00.140
could

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:01.940
be removed.

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:03.300
But it was just such a...

00:19:03.300 --> 00:19:12.160
There are two just beautiful pencil drawings of that project which are extremely...

00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:20.196
He drew all the time and he drew what he saw and he drew the landscape a lot and

00:19:20.196 --> 00:19:21.100
with very

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:29.084
few lines in the kind of greatest sense of a cartoonist actually, with the skill of

00:19:29.084 --> 00:19:29.180
a

00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:34.000
cartoonist who doesn't need many lines to communicate a huge amount of information,

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:40.013
he could do that with landscape and architectural drawing which I think no one else

00:19:40.013 --> 00:19:40.520
has got

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:48.720
close to. How are we to understand Cedric as an enabler or the use of architecture

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:50.120
as an enabler?

00:19:52.760 --> 00:19:57.182
I think Cedric saw it very much in terms of, well he stated it that I think he saw

00:19:57.182 --> 00:19:57.560
it very

00:19:57.560 --> 00:20:01.613
much in terms of the progression of technology and he saw the progression of

00:20:01.613 --> 00:20:02.360
technology was

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:10.196
was smaller, faster, lighter. And that that was about, information technology was

00:20:10.196 --> 00:20:10.680
going

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:17.552
to be the technology that drove humanity from where he was leaving off pretty well.

00:20:17.552 --> 00:20:17.800
And

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:25.494
therefore human beings were going to be making more decisions for themselves. And in

00:20:25.494 --> 00:20:25.860
that

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:30.100
respect he then saw that as a way of

00:20:30.100 --> 00:20:34.100
what about, how does that, what are the

00:20:34.100 --> 00:20:38.360
consequences for architecture then? If human beings, individuals

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:42.120
will make increasingly more and more decisions for themselves, they will not come

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:45.160
from some other governing body.

00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:50.260
And so he tried to design with that

00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:54.300
in mind and that meant always a level of interaction

00:20:54.300 --> 00:21:01.040
between the user of a building and the artefact itself,

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:02.140
or the building itself,

00:21:02.600 --> 00:21:07.860
which allowed the user to determine the use also.

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:11.700
So in the example of Generator, for instance,

00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:17.960
which was a perfect demonstration of that idea,

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:21.080
the buildings could be physically moved,

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:23.920
it could be planned and physically moved around the site

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:26.240
by the users according to their desires.

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:31.160
And you would never, one could never predetermine that

00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:32.440
until it was actually used.

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:35.400
You would never know what the outcome of that building would be

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:37.780
until you started as a user to use it.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:43.280
So I think he saw architecture as an enabler in that respect,

00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:47.240
and as the architect, you then supplied enough kit

00:21:47.240 --> 00:21:50.140
for that to happen, to begin.

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:53.340
I think the relevance of Cedric Price today

00:21:53.340 --> 00:22:04.000
is that he had this idea about human,

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:10.580
more decision-making being made by individuals increasingly

00:22:10.580 --> 00:22:17.260
and how one does that or learns to handle that.

00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:22.900
I think that's the most useful thing that Cedric talked about.

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:26.040
and was interested in.

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:31.560
And then what the consequences of architecture can do

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:33.020
in relation to that.

00:22:33.680 --> 00:22:37.400
But his fascination in human beings, I think,

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:39.760
is what is relevant about Cedric,

00:22:39.760 --> 00:22:45.260
and I think what the industry has a huge amount still to learn.

00:22:47.780 --> 00:22:51.180
And one can learn it by reading his writing.

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:52.940
It just is hard work.

00:22:53.340 --> 00:22:59.180
He was so cryptic in so many ways that you have to work twice as hard as he did

00:22:59.180 --> 00:23:01.000
and he worked really, really hard.

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:02.840
Really hard.

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:08.380
The archive is testament to the amount of work he did

00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:10.580
and the amount of work he...

00:23:10.580 --> 00:23:14.420
what he demanded from his clients

00:23:14.420 --> 00:23:17.180
and the people that worked for him and with him

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:18.800
and his readers.

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:22.360
When you read, he demands that you pay attention

00:23:22.360 --> 00:23:27.200
and also that you think for yourself and you take from...

00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:29.200
Same with the drawings. Drawings and the text.

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:32.320
You take what's useful for you and then you go with it.

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:34.880
He doesn't want you to copy it or...

00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:37.280
You have to use it to think for yourself.
